# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. Only the NEXT_PUBLIC_* values belong in this
# file at all — see the note under "Better Auth" below for why the secrets do
# not, and where they go instead.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL and NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_SITE_URL are needed at *build*
# time, not just at runtime: `convexBetterAuthNextJs` (lib/auth-server.ts)
# validates them when the module loads, which happens while Next.js collects
# page data. `bun run build` fails with "CONVEX_SITE_URL is not set" without
# them. Run `bunx convex dev` once to have both written for you.

# --- Convex (written by `bunx convex dev`) ---
CONVEX_DEPLOYMENT=<> # team: <>, project: <>

# The deployment's API URL, used by the browser client. …convex.cloud
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL=<>

# The deployment's *site* URL, used for auth routes. …convex.site
# Different host to the one above; Better Auth is served from the site URL.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_SITE_URL=<>

# Where this app is served from. Used by next.config.ts for the /api/auth
# rewrite and by the auth client.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Better Auth (Google + GitHub OAuth, multi-user) ---
# Better Auth runs *inside* Convex, so these are set on the Convex deployment
# rather than read from this file. A value here would simply not be seen:
#
#   bunx convex env set BETTER_AUTH_SECRET "$(openssl rand -base64 32)"
#   bunx convex env set SITE_URL http://localhost:3000
#   bunx convex env set GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID <>
#   bunx convex env set GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET <>
#   bunx convex env set GITHUB_CLIENT_ID <>
#   bunx convex env set GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET <>
#
# SITE_URL doubles as the trusted origin for CORS (convex/betterAuth/auth.ts),
# so it has to be the real deployed origin in production, not localhost.
#
# Both OAuth callbacks point at the *Convex site* URL, not the Next.js one —
# Better Auth's callback is served by Convex:
#   https://<deployment>.convex.site/api/auth/callback/google
#   https://<deployment>.convex.site/api/auth/callback/github
#
# Google:  Cloud Console > APIs & Services > Credentials > OAuth 2.0 Client ID
#          (type: Web application)
# GitHub:  Settings > Developer settings > OAuth Apps > New OAuth App
#
# Anyone may sign in — Bundestrack is multi-user by design. An account is
# optional and only stores custom score weightings and followed MPs.

# --- Pipeline ingestion ---
# Shared key the Python pipeline presents as `Authorization: Bearer <key>` when
# POSTing to /api/pipeline/* (convex/http.ts). Also set on Convex, not here:
#
#   bunx convex env set PIPELINE_API_KEY "$(openssl rand -base64 32)"
#
# The pipeline reads the same value from its own environment; see
# pipeline/README.md.
